19:T61a,## 1. Requirements

- An **N×N board** (3×3 by default), every cell starts empty.
- **Two players** alternate turns; each owns a mark (X or O).
- A turn **places a mark** in an empty cell.
- After each move, **detect a win** (a full row, column, or diagonal) or a **draw** (board full, no winner).
- **Reject illegal moves**, out of bounds, occupied cell, or playing out of turn.

## 2. The classes

- `Game`the **orchestrator**. Holds the two players, tracks whose turn it is, and exposes `move(r, c)`.
- `Board`a **grid of Cells**. Knows how to `place` a mark and `winner()`.
- `Player`a person with a `Symbol`.
- `Symbol`an **enum**: `X`, `O`, `EMPTY`.

## 3. Where does win-checking live?

Keep it in the **`Board`**, it owns the grid, so it's the natural place to scan rows, columns, and diagonals. If the rules need to vary (different win conditions, board shapes), pull it out into a pluggable **`WinStrategy`**, but for plain tic-tac-toe a method on `Board` is plenty. **Don't over-engineer.**

## 4. Extensibility

Because the board takes an `n`, the same model is an **N×N** game for free. Add more players by making `players` a list and the turn a rotating index. A `WinStrategy` that checks "4 in a row" turns it into **Connect-4** with the rest reused.

## 5. A note on modelling

Model the board as a **grid of `Cell`s** (each cell holds a `Symbol`), and use an **enum for the mark** rather than separate `X` and `O` classes, they'd carry no behaviour, just an identity, which is exactly what an enum is for.

```
enum Symbol { X, O, EMPTY }
```c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-case-studies","activeLesson":"tic-tac-toe"}],["$","$L18","tic-tac-toe",{"lesson":{"id":"tic-tac-toe","patternId":"lld-case-studies","title":"Design Tic-Tac-Toe","kind":"case-study","difficulty":"Intro","statement":"A clean OO model of a turn-based board game, board, players, moves, and win detection, designed so it generalises to N×N or other grid games.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Adobe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Model the board","code":["enum Symbol { X, O, EMPTY }","","class Board {","    Cell[][] cells;","    int n;","    void place(int r, int c, Symbol s) { ... }","    Symbol winner() { ... }   // scan rows/cols/diagonals","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Game orchestrates the match, it holds the two players, tracks whose turn it is, and exposes move(). It owns the rules and turn order, nothing else.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"game","name":"Game","fields":["- players","- turn"],"methods":["+ move(r,c)"],"x":0,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"board","name":"Board","fields":["- cells","- n"],"methods":["+ place(r,c,s)","+ winner()"],"x":1.2,"y":0.2},{"id":"player","name":"Player","fields":["- symbol: Symbol"],"x":2.4,"y":0.2},{"id":"cell","name":"Cell","fields":["- symbol: Symbol"],"x":0.6,"y":1.55},{"id":"sym","name":"Symbol","stereotype":"enum","fields":["X","O","EMPTY"],"x":1.9,"y":1.55,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"r1","from":"game","to":"board","kind":"aggregate","label":"board"},{"id":"r2","from":"game","to":"player","kind":"aggregate","label":"players 2"},{"id":"r3","from":"board","to":"cell","kind":"compose","label":"cells n×n"},{"id":"r4","from":"cell","to":"sym","kind":"depend"},{"id":"r5","from":"player","to":"sym","kind":"depend"}],"shownClassIds":["game","player"],"shownRelIds":["r2"],"activeIds":["game"]},"vars":[["Game","players + turn"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"The Board is a grid of Cells; it places a Symbol and checks for a winner. The mark is an ENUM (X / O / EMPTY), not an X class and an O class, which would carry no behaviour.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["game","board","player","cell","sym"],"shownRelIds":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5"],"activeIds":["board","cell","sym"]},"vars":[["Board","cells n×n"],["Symbol","enum, not subclasses ✓"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"Tiny, five pieces, and it generalises: Board takes n for an N×N grid, and win-checking lives in one place (the Board, or a WinStrategy if rules must vary). Connect-4 reuses almost all of it.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["game","board","player","cell","sym"],"shownRelIds":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["scales","N×N · more players"],["win-check","Board → WinStrategy"]]}]},{"label":"Flow: make a move","code":["void move(int r, int c) {","    requireTurn(); requireEmpty(r, c);   // reject illegal","    board.place(r, c, current.symbol);","    if (board.winner() == EMPTY) turn = other();","}"],"steps":[{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"The current Player makes a move at (1,1). The Game validates it: is it their turn, and is the cell empty? If not, the move is rejected.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"p","label":"Player","kind":"actor"},{"id":"g","label":"Game","tint":"accent"},{"id":"b","label":"Board"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"p","to":"g","label":"move(1,1)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"g","to":"b","label":"place(1,1, X)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"g","to":"b","label":"winner()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m4","from":"b","to":"g","label":"none yet","kind":"return"},{"id":"m5","from":"g","to":"p","label":"next turn: O","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["check","turn + empty cell"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"Game tells the Board to place X at (1,1), then asks the Board to check for a winner. The Board owns the grid, so it owns the row/column/diagonal scan.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["place","X @ (1,1)"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"No winner yet → Game flips the turn to O. The Game owns the rules and turn order; the Board owns the grid and win-check, clean separation, and easy to extend.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":5,"activeIndex":4},"vars":[["Game","turn order + rules"],["Board","grid + win check"]]}]}]}}]]}]
